-equality and later-writer arms -> after: the settled filter excludes the same records, an owner row carries a held-MEANING record out of the amendment row, and the rationale says the carried record is moved, not hidden: it stays on the approval brief as drift and the owner's item names it -> not the same: a record the old text minted now leaves the amendment row. Ready to bless:\n  - The new sentence is SR-178's half of the routing. The rationale shows that the drift a carried record holds is still reported, so moving the record off the amendment row hides nothing.\n  - The rename confines \"only\" to the settled filter.\n  - TC-147 now verifies this row: `test_a_held_row_leaves_the_amendment_row_beside_a_released_one` passes.\n\n  Note, not a return: the carry is also stated twice in LLR-153 (\"Those rows leave the amendment row\", \"and the rows the owner row carries\"). LLR-153's re-sit may want to leave this row as the drift-side statement and keep one routing statement there.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-147 Method -> before: the intake mint arms, with no held-act trigger -> after: the same plus the held-act clause. It mints one owner row and item per landing and registry, blessed or not, retired rows included. Each minted command run through the real act exits 0, anchors its rows and records no verdict. A second landing's row refuses the narrow command, and option (a)'s widened act exits 0. CLARITY, a released rung, an equal copy, an unaccepted verdict, off-bound text and replay mint nothing. The non-TOML refusal and its recovery are checked, the latest accepted verdict decides, and carried rows leave the amendment row -> not the same: many new arms. Ready to bless:\n  - Each clause has a named case in `tests/test_intake_held_act.py`, and all of them pass.\n  - The row is Full-tier on real git repositories, as its tier says.\n  - Its Verifies now names LLR-364 and SR-178 for the carried-record sentence.\n  - It already asserts the refusal that LLR-153 is returned to state, so it needs no change when LLR-153 re-sits.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=7\n\n## Dispositions\n\n```toml\ntitle = \"LLR-153 amendment return: state trigger (d)'s non-TOML open-items refusal and name the actor who widens the owner act\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsafety_class = \"spine\"\nbuildtier = \"quick\"\npriority = 5\nspecref = \"docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-174\", \"SR-228\"]\nneeds = [\"WI-993\"]\n```\n\nThis continues `docs/reviews/wi-993/007-ADJUDICATE-8b86c0d.md` (`VERDICT: MEANING rows=7`). LLR-153 is plain MEANING and is not blessed. The other six rows are MEANING+BLESS. The build is not in question: its tests pass and agree with what TC-147 asserts. The fix is one cell, LLR-153 `detail`, and needs no code change:\n\n1. **State the refusal.** Where a landing owes an open item, from trigger (d) or from a drafted `[open_item]` table, and the open-items registry is not the TOML carrier, the mint refuses by naming the carrier. It writes nothing, including any amendment row the same landing owes, and the merge stands. Recovery is converting the carrier and then sweeping the range. Reconcile \"Every other state writes nothing\" so it no longer reads as if no landing is refused.\n2. **Name the actor.** Replace \"any other row of it unanchored when the owner acts is named beside them\" with a sentence naming the actor: the item's option (a) directs the owner to name any such row beside them.\n3. **Optional.** Keep one routing statement of the carry in LLR-153 (\"Those rows leave the amendment row\" and the trigger (a) exclusion now say it twice), and leave LLR-364's sentence as the drift-side statement.\n\nThen re-sit LLR-153 ahead of any act that copies the design registry. LLR-118, LLR-299 and LLR-364 keep their blessings unless their text moves. TC-147 already asserts the refusal and needs no edit.\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment\n\r\n[design.LLR-153]\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-174\", \"SR-228\"]\r\ntitle = \"The unified trunk-side intake mint + the context block + the approval-level arms\"\r\nmodule = \"project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py;project-trajectory/scripts/acceptance_record.py\"\r\ncode_symbol = \"\"\"intake_after_merge/_amendment_drafts/_latest_rulings/_held_act_drafts/_held_act_draft/mint_gap_rows/parse_dispositions/parse_disposition_section/next_wi_id/context_block/_inject_open_item/_pending_oi_lines/adjudication_action/flip_verified/_standing_successors/_title_identity_refusal/_repoint_owed/unanchored_rows/text_as_bound\"\"\"\r\ndetail = \"\"\"The mint invariant: a WI id is created only by a human trunk commit or this helper - lanes never mint. next_wi_id counts from max(the docs/id-watermark mark, the sweep of every spec FILENAME under docs/work/ and docs/archive/work/, active/<branch>/ included) + 1 - never max(live) alone, so an id freed by a deleted spec is never re-issued - and trace.read_watermark's refusal on an absent or malformed mark is deliberately NOT caught: a mint with no record of what has been allocated must not proceed on a guess (TC-158, IF-101). Four triggers, serial by construction: (a) the approved/routed-cell diff on the merged commit via check_trajectory.staged_spine_amendments, whose walk covers the SR, LLR, TC, SN, DA and SUR tiers -> one adjudication row listing each changed row/cell/before-after (routed traced cells: SR SN-Refs and Boundary-Refs, LLR SR-Refs and TC Verifies per the declared cell split); (b) a merged spec carrying ## Handback -> the disposition row (outcomes cancel / defer / re-queue with drafted follow-up / surface an open item; NEVER minted for an adjudication row - no recursion, and handback.hand_back refuses the act itself); (c) the dispatcher's gap census -> concrete gap-closure rows, deduped against every existing row; (d) an accepted verdict in the merged range whose latest amendment ruling on a row is MEANING, blessed or not, on a rung the dial holds, with the row at the range's end differing from its approved-snapshot copy and either equal to the text the verdict's binding records or gone from its registry, yields, per landing and per registry, one queued owner row and one pending open item naming every such row of that registry, their verdicts and the act's command, the item cited in that row's needs, in the mint's one commit. The command re-attests every row it names and never names a verdict, the act being the owner's, and the item states that the act copies the whole registry, so any other row of it unanchored when the owner acts is named beside them. Those rows leave the amendment row. Every other state writes nothing, and a row left over, one whose text differs from what its verdict bound or whose verdict bound none included, stays on the amendment row as any other record does. A row sat more than once reads at its latest accepted verdict in the range. A replay mints no second item. Drafts-not-mints: a merged adjudication row's ## Dispositions fenced-toml drafts mint at ITS merge, validated loudly; a draft carrying an [open_item] table also mints a pending open item from it, with no work-item pointer, and writes the item's id into that draft's needs, so the minted row is the item's queued placeholder and reads blocked until the ruling, and a draft with no SpecRef gets the item's own registry record as its SpecRef. Tier signals are measurable (rows touched, gate delta, handback reason class). context_block renders the pure registry joins (cancelled precedent WITH REASONS first, the pending OIs cited in needs by the row, its predecessors or its siblings, the LLR/TC code map, CMP knowledge packs, IF seams, precedent reviews) - advisory-never-gating, three consumers; an open item's wi_refs joins nothing. flip_verified resolves the hold from the approval dial: held prints a recommendation and writes nothing; released requests the flip, but _apply_flips skips an already Approved row and refuses every other Status by name, so it writes nothing; an unreadable dial or stage reads as held. adjudication_action(human_held, verdict) returns flip when released, reattest when held with a CLARITY verdict, and recommend otherwise. The mint commit mirrors the claim's bookkeeping shape; every derived title is deterministic, so the sweep CLI re-run is idempotent. An adopts-only disposition block allocates no id; a standing row found by exact title whose lineage (its supersedes) differs from the draft's refuses the mint before any write, while one on the draft's own lineage stands; and every replay re-points to the full successor set. Trigger (a) excludes only records the merged range already settled under LLR-364 and the rows the owner row carries.\"\"\"\r\nrationale = \"\"\"A detected event must FORCE a row into the registry with nobody watching, and an id two lanes could pick at once must stay unrepresentable (R1).\"\"\"\r\ntest_refs = \"(see TC-147)\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nphase = 4\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
